Advanced Malignancy (Solid Tumours)
Palliative & End of Life
Unintentional weight loss, Intractable progressive pain, Profound asthenia/weakness

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-004
Reason for Visit: Unmanageable visceral pain and severe asthenia leaving the patient unable to mobilize to the bathroom.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, daughter
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a severe escalation in abdominal pain over the last six hours, paired with sudden, profound generalized weakness that caused a near-fall. The patient's inability to manage pain orally and sheer physical collapse required an urgent after-hours medical assessment to prevent further injury and establish immediate subcutaneous symptom control.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The pain in my stomach is tearing me apart, and my legs just completely gave way when I tried to stand."
History of Present Illness: Over the past few months, the patient has experienced unintentional severe weight loss completely out of proportion to caloric intake, alongside severe cachexia and visible muscle wasting. Anorexia with prominent early satiety has been a constant issue. Over the last week, there has been a rapid deterioration of physical performance status. Today, the patient developed intractable and progressively worsening visceral pain in the abdomen. This was accompanied by profound systemic asthenia and generalized disabling weakness, culminating in a collapse when attempting to stand. The patient has palpable, fixed, and hard metastatic tissue masses in the abdomen. Mild dyspnea and ascites secondary to local tumor compression are also reported. There is progressive psychosocial and existential distress concerning end-of-life care.
Functional Status: Acutely deteriorated; now requires a two-person assist to stand.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk. Daughter is anxious and unable to physically lift the patient alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 96% on room air | Wt: 52 Kg
General: Frail, visibly wasted, lying in bed holding abdomen, grimacing in pain.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals a distended, tense abdomen with shifting dullness indicative of ascites, and palpable, hard, irregular masses in the right upper quadrant. Neurological exam shows profound, generalized symmetrical weakness (Grade 3/5 in lower limbs) without focal localizing signs. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed via noted unintentional weight loss (loose clothing, cachexia), observation of intractable progressive pain (grimacing, diaphoresis), and profound asthenia/weakness (inability to lift legs against gravity).
Environmental Observations: Walkways are clear, but the bathroom is located far from the bedroom, increasing fall risk given current weakness.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has been vomiting oral sustained-release morphine; therefore, analgesic absorption is compromised.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Advanced Malignancy (Solid Tumours) - Status: Exacerbated (Pain Crisis & Asthenia)
Differentials:

Bowel perforation - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of board-like abdominal rigidity, rebound tenderness, or signs of acute peritonitis/septic shock.

Spinal cord compression - Ruled out/unlikely due to intact (though weak) bilateral sensation, preserved reflexes, and intact sphincter tone.

Acute dehydration - Ruled out/unlikely to be the sole cause of weakness, given the preserved skin turgor and moist mucous membranes.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient has suffered an acute physical collapse and unmanageable pain crisis driven by advanced solid organ malignancy and compromised oral analgesic absorption due to vomiting. The objective findings of severe visceral pain, tense ascites, and profound systemic asthenia render the patient completely unable to mobilize safely. Delaying medical assessment until regular clinic hours would consign the patient to hours of agonizing, unmedicated pain and a high risk of traumatic injury from unassisted falls. Urgent after-hours intervention was absolutely necessary to bypass the oral route, administer immediate subcutaneous analgesia, and arrange urgent functional support.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat subcutaneous hydromorphone for the pain crisis and subcutaneous haloperidol for nausea. Converted oral analgesia regimen to a continuous subcutaneous infusion via syringe driver.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the need to use the toilet (trigger), you must ring the bell for your daughter and use the bedside commode (behavior) to ensure you do not fall and injure yourself (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral for community nursing to set up the syringe driver and occupational therapy for a bedside commode.
DME/Safety (Optional): Commode and a hospital bed with pressure-relieving mattress.
Follow-up: Community nursing review in the morning; GP follow-up in 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops sudden, severe, unremitting back pain, loss of bowel/bladder control, or if the abdominal pain becomes severe and unrelenting despite subcutaneous breakthrough medication, the family must call the palliative care hotline or 000 immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The transition to subcutaneous medications is to ensure symptom control, not necessarily an indication of imminent death in the next 24 hours, but reflects disease progression.
Clinical Course: The extreme weakness is likely irreversible and represents the natural progression of the advanced cancer. Focus must shift entirely to comfort and fall prevention.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk under any circumstances. Use the call bell for assistance. Call the triage nurse if breakthrough meds do not work within 30 minutes.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because you are vomiting, the pain pills are not absorbing. Giving the medication under the skin ensures it gets into your system quickly and reliably to stop the pain.
Actionable Steps: Accept assistance for all transfers. Use the breakthrough medication proactively before care interventions like washing or repositioning.

